@@ -0,0 +1,95 @@
+# Closing-keyword gate (#8183)
+#
+# Closes the gap behind a real incident: PR #2949 merged into `main` with a body
+# ending in `Closes #2892` — the keyword inside an INLINE CODE SPAN — so GitHub
+# never closed #2892. The issue stayed OPEN with a `P0-blocker` label for a
+# month, its fix already in `main`, until a lane was dispatched at it on
+# 2026-10-11 to fix an already-fixed defect.
+#
+# WHY A DEDICATED WORKFLOW (not a job in ci.yml / python-ci.yml)
+# --------------------------------------------------------------
+# The remedy is a body EDIT, and a `pull_request` trigger only fires `opened`,
+# `synchronize` and `reopened` by default — so a gate in ci.yml would go red,
+# the author would fix the body, and nothing would re-run: red forever, with no
+# action that clears it. Adding `edited` to ci.yml's trigger would re-run the
+# WHOLE ~20-gate matrix (and python-ci's sharded suite) on every body edit — an
+# enormous cost for a text check.
+#
+# A workflow whose trigger is `types: [opened, edited, synchronize, reopened]`
+# gets the re-trigger for free and costs ~10s of one hosted runner: stdlib-only
+# Python, no DB, no services, no matrix, no node. That is the cheap half of the
+# fix; the check itself is `tools/pr_close_keyword.py`.
+#
+# WHAT IT CHECKS
+# --------------
+# It strips exactly what GitHub ignores (fenced code blocks, inline code spans)
+# and then requires: IF the body declares a closing keyword anywhere, at least
+# one instance must SURVIVE the strip. A body with no keyword (`Refs #N`) is
+# untouched — not every PR closes an issue.
+#
+# Evidence the rule is GitHub's, not ours: #2949 (code-span keyword, never
+# closed) and #8669 (same shape; GitHub closed its issue only because a COMMIT
+# MESSAGE carried a bare keyword). Re-measured over the last 298 merged PRs on
+# 2026-10-19: 172 carry a live bare keyword, exactly 1 body is inert (#8669),
+# 125 declare none — the convention is followed almost always, which is why the
+# failure is rare and silent rather than obvious.
+#
+# NOT (YET) A REQUIRED CHECK. It reports; branch protection does not wait on it.
+# Promoting it to required means changing the live branch protection, `.mergify.yml`
+# and the declarative settings mirror TOGETHER (pinned by
+# tests/test_required_set_sync.py) — the owner's call, tracked on #8183 as the
+# remaining half.
+#
+# The mirror is described, not named (#5215 Task 4): that guard's reader scan is a
+# BYTE scan, so spelling the path in prose here is indistinguishable from this
+# workflow reading it — the repo already paid for that lesson once, and the fix is
+# to not spell it, never to exempt the file.
+name: PR closing keyword
+
+on:
+  pull_request:
+    types: [opened, edited, synchronize, reopened]
+
+permissions:
+  contents: read
+
+# Per-PR group: one in-flight run per PR, superseded on the next push or body
+# edit. `cancel-in-progress: true` is right here because the run has no side
+# effect — it only reads the body of the head it was triggered for. Fan-out is
+# bounded by the number of open PRs itself (one run per head, like
+# `ai-review-gate`), so the per-unit key cannot flood the pool the way #9115 did.
+concurrency:
+  group: pr-close-keyword-${{ github.event.pull_request.number }}
+  cancel-in-progress: true
+
+jobs:
+  closing-keyword:
+    # Bound like `manifest-integrity`: a stalled runner must not hold a check on
+    # every PR open. The work is seconds; 5 minutes is a failure signal, not a
+    # budget.
+    timeout-minutes: 5
+    # A VERSION-PINNED hosted label, not `ubuntu-latest`: on this repo
+    # `ubuntu-latest` also matches the self-hosted ARM64 pool (#7867), and this
+    # job should be deterministic — it reads one string and exits.
+    runs-on: ubuntu-24.04
+    steps:
+      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
+      - uses: actions/setup-python@v5
+        with:
+          python-version: '3.12'
+      - name: Reject a closing keyword GitHub cannot act on (#8183)
+        # The body arrives through `env:`, NOT interpolated into `run:` — a PR
+        # body pasted into a shell script is arbitrary shell (and would also
+        # trip the repo's workflow-secret/expression guards).
+        env:
+          PR_BODY: ${{ github.event.pull_request.body }}
+        run: |
+          # Deliberately NOT named `pr-` + `body.md`: that basename is a ROOT file
+          # on the #6784 non-python allowlist, and tests/test_ci_selection.py reds on
+          # ANY in-tree file that names it — a temp filename sharing the name would
+          # read as a reader of that root file and break the allowlist invariant.
+          printf '%s' "$PR_BODY" > "$RUNNER_TEMP/description.md"
+          # No `|| true`, no `; exit 0`: a non-zero exit from the guard IS the
+          # failure. Exit 1 = inert keyword; exit 2 = could not read the body,
+          # which is never a pass either.
+          python3 tools/pr_close_keyword.py --body-file "$RUNNER_TEMP/description.md"
